            {
              "text": "{\"problem\": {\"theorem\": \"Let \\\\( x \\\\) and \\\\( y \\\\) be positive integers. If for each positive integer \\\\( n \\\\) we have that\\n\\n\\\\[\\n(n y)^{2}+1 \\\\mid x^{\\\\varphi(n)}-1\\n\\\\]\\n\\nprove that \\\\( x=1 \\\\).\", \"assumptions\": [], \"domain\": \"bmo\", \"explicit_subgoals\": []}, \"candidate_text\": \"Assume for contradiction that \\\\(x>1\\\\). Taking \\\\(n=2\\\\), since \\\\(\\\\varphi(2)=1\\\\), gives\\n\\\\[\\n4y^2+1\\\\mid x-1.\\n\\\\]\\nChoose a prime \\\\(p\\\\mid 4y^2+1\\\\). Then \\\\(p\\\\) is odd, \\\\(p\\\\nmid y\\\\), and \\\\((2y)^2\\\\equiv-1\\\\pmod p\\\\), so \\\\(p\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod4\\\\). In particular, \\\\(p\\\\mid x-1\\\\). Put\\n\\\\[\\ns=v_p(x-1),\\\\qquad K=s+1.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nWe construct a positive integer \\\\(n\\\\) such that\\n\\\\[\\np^K\\\\mid n^2y^2+1\\\\quad\\\\text{and}\\\\quad p\\\\nmid\\\\varphi(n).\\n\\\\]\\nLet \\\\(f(T)=y^2T^2+1\\\\). We have \\\\(f(2)\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod p\\\\) and\\n\\\\[\\nf'(2)=4y^2\\\\not\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod p.\\n\\\\]\\nHence Hensel lifting gives a unit \\\\(r\\\\pmod{p^K}\\\\), with \\\\(r\\\\equiv2\\\\pmod p\\\\), such that \\\\(f(r)\\\\equiv0\\\\pmod{p^K}\\\\).\\n\\nWe also need a convenient representative of this residue class. Choose a primitive root \\\\(g\\\\) modulo \\\\(p\\\\). One of \\\\(g\\\\) and \\\\(g+p\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p^2\\\\): if \\\\(g^{p-1}\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod{p^2}\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\n(g+p)^{p-1}\\\\equiv g^{p-1}+(p-1)g^{p-2}p\\\\not\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod{p^2}.\\n\\\\]\\nCall the resulting integer \\\\(a\\\\). It is a primitive root modulo every \\\\(p^j\\\\): indeed, \\\\(v_p(a^{p-1}-1)=1\\\\), so LTE gives\\n\\\\[\\nv_p\\\\bigl(a^{(p-1)m}-1\\\\bigr)=1+v_p(m).\\n\\\\]\\nMoreover, \\\\(1<a<2p\\\\), \\\\(p\\\\nmid a\\\\), and \\\\(a\\\\not\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\). The prime-factor formula for \\\\(\\\\varphi(a)\\\\) then shows \\\\(p\\\\nmid\\\\varphi(a)\\\\): otherwise some prime divisor \\\\(q\\\\mid a\\\\) would satisfy \\\\(q\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\); since \\\\(q\\\\le a<2p\\\\), this would force \\\\(q=a\\\\equiv1\\\\pmod p\\\\), a contradiction.\\n\\nBecause \\\\(a\\\\) is a primitive root modulo \\\\(p^K\\\\), choose \\\\(e\\\\ge1\\\\) such that \\\\(a^e\\\\equiv r\\\\pmod{p^K}\\\\), and set \\\\(n=a^e\\\\). Then\\n\\\\[\\np^K\\\\mid n^2y^2+1,\\n\\\\]\\nwhile\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\varphi(n)=\\\\varphi(a^e)=a^{e-1}\\\\varphi(a)\\n\\\\]\\nis not divisible by \\\\(p\\\\).\\n\\nApplying the hypothesis to this \\\\(n\\\\) yields\\n\\\\[\\np^K\\\\mid x^{\\\\varphi(n)}-1.\\n\\\\]\\nHowever, because \\\\(p\\\\) is odd, \\\\(p\\\\mid x-1\\\\), and \\\\(p\\\\nmid\\\\varphi(n)\\\\), LTE gives\\n\\\\[\\nv_p\\\\bigl(x^{\\\\varphi(n)}-1\\\\bigr)\\n=v_p(x-1)+v_p(\\\\varphi(n))=s<K,\\n\\\\]\\na contradiction. Thus \\\\(x>1\\\\) is impossible. Since \\\\(x\\\\) is a positive integer, \\\\(x=1\\\\).\"}",
              "text_elements": [],
              "type": "text"
            }
          ],
